More specifically the gradients can be too large out in the tails,
or the large gradients can frustrate adaptation.  This information
can also be built into the model directly by standardizing the
parameters, i.e.  from

parameters {
  real theta;
}

to

transformed data {
  real mu;
  real sigma;
  mu = ;
  sigma ;
}

parameters {
  real theta_tilde;
}

transformed parameters {
  real theta;
  theta = mu + sigma * theta_tilde;

}

On May 2, 2016, at 9:12 PM, Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:

> The other time that you might need inits is if the parameters are far from unit scale, for example if the posterior distribution is centered at 100 with a posterior sd of 1, or if the posterior distribution is centered at .01 with a posterior sd of .01.  In these settings, default random inits can be so far from the meat of the posterior dist that Nuts can have trouble getting there.
>
> (Another item for the manual, I guess…)
>
