Message-ID: <bz_5TeuGi4o.NZhVLxX0CQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <bz_5TeuGi4o.8FG4VMqhCQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <bz_5TeuGi4o.Tcg8F6agCQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <bz_5TeuGi4o.8FG4VMqhCQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/bz_5TeuGi4o/m/NZhVLxX0CQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

That's a warning, by the way, not an error.  It can
arise from numerical instability.    The double exp()
is bad news, exp(1 - exp(x)/mu) is very likely to
underflow to zero.

We have an alternative parameterization on the log
scale of neg_binomial_2_log().  That might be more
stable in this case, but you'd have to do some algebra
to get everything onto the log scale.

You can also try to debug it with print() statements to
figure out what the values are and where the zero input
is coming from.

- Bob

> On May 4, 2016, at 12:59 AM, Swupnil Sahai <swu...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> I'm still having the issue after adding parameter constraints (updated code is attached). Once again the issue disappears if I remove the "/mu_nk" from line 76.
>
> And I'm using a Macintosh (Late 2014 Macbook Pro).
>
> On Tuesday, May 3, 2016 at 8:54:01 PM UTC-7, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> We print line numbers so that users can figure out where
> the error's coming from.  That's why we don't recommend including
> models as quoted strings (also, it rules out either quotes for
> printing or the transposition operator depending on which quote you choose).
>
> All of your errors are due to missing constraints in the
> parameter declarations.
