Subject: Re: [stan-users] Eigenvalue of non-symmetric matrix in Rstan
Message-ID: <ZsombXFQeFE.g4CwiiZPCgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <ZsombXFQeFE.t2Oo-U1HCgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <ZsombXFQeFE.v-cfllxACgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <ZsombXFQeFE.t2Oo-U1HCgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/ZsombXFQeFE/m/g4CwiiZPCgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Can’t you just construct A^{T}A (or A^{T} A) directly, take the eigendecompositon
of that, and take the square-root of any positive eigenvectors directly?

The problem with anything else is that we need to compute gradients in Stan,
so we can’t just introduce arbitrary numerical algorithms that may not have
clean gradients.  If this eigenvalue will appear only as data, then you can
compute it externally and pass it into the Stan program.

On May 6, 2016, at 7:27 AM, Jessica Wong <ytw...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Dear Ben
>
> Thank you for your quick response. As I am sure that the eigenvalue that I am going to get from the non-symmetric matrix is real, is it possible to call the eigen() function from R, or make a user-defined function? Or do you have other possible suggestions?
>
> Thanks and regards
> Jessica
>
> On Friday, 6 May 2016 14:20:10 UTC+8, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> > On Friday, May 6, 2016 at 12:19:52 AM UTC-4, Jessica Wong wrote:
> > > I am trying to get the eigenvalue for a non-symmetric matrix. In R, we can use the eigen() function. However, I can only find a similar function in Rstan for only symmetric matrix call eigenvales_sym(). Any suggestions how I can get that eigenvalue in Rstan?
> >
> > In the non-symmetric case, you can't guarantee the eigenvalue is real and Stan doesn't do complex numbers.
> >
> > Ben
> >
>

