On Friday, May 6, 2016 at 4:05:10 PM UTC-4, Linas Mockus wrote:
> Hi,
>
> Can somebody advice on the size of nonlinear mixed effects model that could be solved in Stan in reasonable time? For example items that are manufactured in batches exhibit intra-batch variability (items within same batch are different) while average for each batch is also different. This could be handled by two level hierarchical model

Well, I'm currently working with a model that runs in about 8 (?) hours that includes five levels (roughly 77, 292, 2299, 30, and 2300 units per level) for each of three key parameters.  The last two levels are a batch effect and a batch x group interaction.

> and thus the number of parameters to estimate grow with increasing  # of batches. I wonder how many batches one may reasonably consider and are there any tricks to overcome dimensionality explosion problem.

After long enough you know the batch-to-batch variance and can start dropping early batches.

> For instance, nonlinear mixed effects model might involve solution of ODEs.

This I don't know.

Krzysztof

>
> Thank you,
> Linas

