Well, that makes sense. I'm operating on 640x480 images, so..yeah. Just the storage requirement for the image comes down to ~4GB based on what you mentioned.

On Wednesday, 11 May 2016 12:45:10 UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> How many dimensions do you have?  At the very least,
> it's going to require N^2 * 20 bytes plus about 40
> bytes per operator/function call in computing the function
> and its gradient.
>
> The reason to use autodiff is that finite differences
> have low precision and you need to nest one inside the other
> to calculate Hessians.  We only use the finite diffs to
> validate our higher-order autodiff code---we don't recommend
> using them for real work.
>
> The hessian() code does call recover_memory() after each row
> is computed.  We should probably modify it to define H(i,j) = H(j,i)
> just to make sure everything's symmetric.
>
> - Bob
>
> > On May 11, 2016, at 12:20 PM, Kumar Shaurya Shankar <kumar....@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Yes, just the one call to the hessian. The memory growth ramps up the first time my cost function is called, and then remains more or less constant through the subsequent calls to the cost function.
> >
> > Also, the hessian I obtain by autodiff vs the one obtained using finite differences is drastically different (i.e. the Hessian goes from negative definite (as expected) with the autodiff to a hessian with mixed eigen values). The gradients are pretty close, however.
> >
> > On Wednesday, 11 May 2016 12:10:20 UTC-4, Daniel Lee wrote:
> > Is that for one call to hessian? There is some management to do with regards to the autodiff stack. We haven't really pushed on the forward mode, so it's possible we haven't correctly reused the allocated memory or we weren't clear about what needs to happen between iterations to reset memory.
> >
> > If it's multiple calls, can you see if the memory footprint grows and doesn't shrink?
> >
> > On May 11, 2016, at 12:02 PM, Kumar Shaurya Shankar <kumar....@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
