Subject: How to check if a matrix is positive definite or not
Message-ID: <ozV4U1tM-l8.LNxXbolMDAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/ozV4U1tM-l8/m/LNxXbolMDAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

I'm in a process of building a large spatial covariance matrix with lots of blocks. There is a correlation parameter (\gamma) for the off diagonal blocks which need to be constrained such that the resulting covariance matrix is positive definite.

Unfortunately, there is no closed form to get the support of \gamma to ensure the positive definiteness. The support is between -1 and 1 with holes inside. What I did in R is during the sampling, if non-positive definiteness is found (catch the error message from a Cholesky decomposition), I will set the joint log density to the smallest number in log scale (i.e. zero density). Then that proposed \gamma will be declined and keep the previous \gamma.

I'm interested in implementing my model in Rstan to check my own coding (it's so easy to make some small bugs when programming my sampling procedure with such a complicated model, and the convergence is not good so far). But I got stuck when dealing with theses issues.

1. I notice that I can calculate the eigenvalues of a matrix and then check if all positive( or simply the first one, since it's been ordered). But I'm wondering if there be some machine error going on if I'm using this method.

2. Also, if I find a good way to check positive definiteness, is there a way for me to set the joint density to 0 (let stan stay with the previous sample and move on)? Or do I have other ways to address my unknown support issue?

Thanks,

Jiang

From u5f8b51633aa2f2a1 at groups.google.com  Thu May 12 21:29:53 2016
From: u5f8b51633aa2f2a1 at groups.google.com (Michael Weylandt)
Date: Thu, 12 May 2016 21:29:53 +0000
Subject: Speeding Up ARMA Model
Message-ID: <Ll48YEnt9Lo.PHH4Ig1QDAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/Ll48YEnt9Lo/m/PHH4Ig1QDAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

I'm trying to implement a general ARMA(p, q) based on the ARMA(1,1)
example in Section 7.4 of the manual.

My current code is below. It gets the right answer, but samples more
slowly than I'd expect. (I don't have any hard comparisons, just a gut
feeling)

Would vectorizing out the loops in the likelihood speed things up or
are those ok? Is there anything else I should check?

Michael

data {
  int<lower=0> AR_ORDER; // Not using p,q b/c too easily confused with
multivariate series
  int<lower=0> MA_ORDER;
  int<lower=1> T;       // Number of observations (length of series)
  real X[T];            // Observed TS
}
parameters {
  real c;               // Constant Term (not TS mean!)
  real AR[AR_ORDER];    // AR Coefficients
  real MA[MA_ORDER];    // MA Coefficients
  real<lower=0> sigma;  // Shock SD (not TS SD!)
}
transformed parameters {
    real mu;
    // Mean of the time series (assuming first-order stationarity)
    // X_t = c + \sum_{i=1}^p phi_i X_{t-i} + \sum_{j=1}^q theta_j
eps_{t-q} + eps_t
    // E[X_t] = E[c + \sum_{i=1}^p phi_i X_{t-i} + \sum_{j=1}^q
theta_j eps_{t-q} + eps_t]
    //        = c + \sum_{i=1}^p phi_i E[X_{t-i}] + \sum_{j=1}^q
theta_j E[eps_{t-q}] + E[eps_t]
    //  mu    = c + \sum_{i=1}^p phi_i mu + \sum_{j=1}^q theta_j 0 + 0
    // ==> mu = c / 1 - sum_{i=1}^p phi_i
    mu <- c / (1-sum(AR));
}
model {
    // Priors
    c ~ normal(0,10);
    AR ~ normal(0,2);
    MA ~ normal(0,2);
    sigma ~ cauchy(0,5);
    // Likelihood
    //
    // Given a ARMA model of the form:
    //
    // X_t = c + \sum_{i=1}^p phi_i X_{t-i} + \sum_{j=1}^q theta_j
eps_{t-q} + eps_t
    //
    // At time t (conditioned on t-1 filtration), the only "random"
element is eps_t,
    // which can be calculated as:
    //
    // eps_t = X_t - (c + \sum_{i=1}^p phi_i X_{t-i} + \sum_{j=1}^q
theta_j eps_{t-q})
    //       = X_t - M
    //
    // Then we have eps_t ~ N(0, sigma)
    {
        real err[T];
        for(t in 1:T){
            // Point prediction for X[t] all info available up to T (exclusive)
            real M;
            M <- c;

            for(k in 1:AR_ORDER){
                if(t - k > 0){
                    M <- M + AR[k] * X[t-k];
                } else {
                    M <- M + AR[k] * mu;
                }
            }
            for(k in 1:MA_ORDER){
                if(t - k > 0){
                    M <- M + MA[k] * err[t-k];
                } // else: M <- M + MA[k] * 0 so just skip
            }
            err[t] <- X[t] - M;
        }
        err ~ normal(0, sigma);
    }
}

