Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Herra and others,

Yes, you're absolutely right. I totally forgot to include the individual constraints and while it wasn't straight forward in Stan, I appear to have found a solution. I've amended my code to constrain the minimums for each individual theta following https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/stan-users/ibEPXA7apAU. It seems to have done the trick. New code is like the following:

parameters {
  ...
 vector<lower=1>[S] theta_raw;  // duration
  ...
}
transformed parameters {
  vector<lower=0>[S] theta;

  theta <- theta_raw .* d;
}
model {
  ...
  increment_log_prob(theta, alpha, sigma);
  ...

  for(n in 1:N) {
    y[n] ~ reflected_beta(lambda, theta[sp[n]]);
  }

}

On Tuesday, May 17, 2016 at 3:02:58 PM UTC-5, Herra Huu wrote:
> I think the problem is here. The support of pdf is [0,theta], so theta has to be larger than the largest observed value of x. So, at least, those constraints should be added to the model (and the priors should be changed accordingly). Also, it's probably a good idea to write down the log-density directly and avoid those pow() calculations, that way it should be more numerically stable and maybe a bit more efficient as well.
>
>
> tiistai 17. toukokuuta 2016 19.31.07 UTC+3 Peter Smits kirjoitti:
> >
> > I need to correct a mistake in my original definition: x values are constrained to be less than or equal to theta, not 1. This, however, does not explain your problem. This appears to happen when values of theta are approximately less than 1. I'll contact the authors and try and figure out what is going on.
> >

