> On May 19, 2016, at 12:22 PM, Charles Driver <cdriv...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Thanks for the input all. It's still not totally clear to me how I can increment the log prob to account for different parameterizations, but it's not so important

I have to disagree --- it's critical to understand what
you're computing and thus to make sure you're computing
what you think you're computing.

I wrote up a case study of a very simple example
here:

http://mc-stan.org/documentation/case-studies/mle-params.html

- Bob

> - just good to understand and confirm that it's expected!
>
> On Thursday, May 12, 2016 at 7:29:05 PM UTC+2, Krzysztof Sakrejda wrote:
> +1 for going in the manual! Krzysztof
>
> On Thursday, May 12, 2016 at 1:19:14 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> Surprising (the result, not that Michael's right about calc!).
> It looks like we're optimizing f in one case and h in the second,
> where
>
>   f(y_raw, mu, sigma,theta) = g(sigma * y_raw + mu, mu, sigma, theta)
>                                + log normal(y_raw | 0, 1)
>
> vs.
>
>   h(y, mu, sigma, theta) = g(y, mu, sigma, theta)
>                            + log normal(y | mu, sigma)
>
>                          = g(y, mu, sigma, theta)
>                            + log normal((y - mu)/sigma | 0, 1)
>                            - log(sigma)
>
> where g is just the rest of the model log density.
> The only difference is the -log(sigma) term, but that will
> cause different densities.
>
> If I did the algebra right, this should go in the manual.
>
> - Bob
>
> > On May 12, 2016, at 12:51 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Because you’re optimizing with respect to theta_tilde, not
> > theta.  It’s a different parameter space (i.e. the centered
> > and non-centered parameterizations aren’t different
> > parameterizations of the same system, they are
> > fundamentally different spaces that can be manipulated
> > into the same system).
> >
> > On May 12, 2016, at 5:43 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
> >
> >> There's no Jacobian involved here, so why would they
> >> have different modes (mu, not mudev, that is)?
> >>
> >> - Bob
> >>
> >>
> >>> On May 12, 2016, at 12:33 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >>>
> >>> It gives the same posterior expectations but will
> >>> yield different modes as they are densities with
> >>> respect to different parameters.  Of course the
> >>> mode is a terrible and shouldn’t be used, in
> >>> which case this sensitivity is irrelevant.
> >>>
> >>> On May 12, 2016, at 5:06 PM, Charles Driver <cdriv...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >>>
> >>>> Right, I also expected that should give the same result, it just, doesn't :) Mu is unconstrained. I went back and unconstrained hmu for the sake of it, now in the noncentered approach it reaches crazy high or low values while optimizing.
> >>>>
> >>>> On Thursday, May 12, 2016 at 5:40:12 PM UTC+2, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> >>>> That should give you the same answer as
> >>>>
> >>>>> vector[hn] mu;
> >>>>> mudev~normal(0,1);
> >>>>> mu<-hmu+mudev*hsd;
> >>>>
> >>>> mu ~ normal(hmu, hsd);
> >>>>
> >>>> assuming you declare mu as an unconstrained parameter.
> >>>>
> >>>> You shouldn't need that lower=0.001 for scales in Stan.  If you
> >>>> do need it, it's indicating a problem with your model and will
> >>>> lead to computational problems trying to sample near the boundary.
> >>>> We also don't recommend upper-bounding scale parameters.
> >>>>
> >>>> - Bob
> >>>>
> >>>>
> >>>>> On May 10, 2016, at 2:14 PM, Charles Driver <cdriv...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >>>>>
> >>>>> I was surprised that in the model I've been working on, whenever I optimized it, the hyper variances ran out to the upper bound very quickly. To understand what was going on I set up a toy in R, which indeed shows that while point estimates of centered hyper variances are in general too low, those of noncentered variances appear unbounded in their desire to be large...
> >>>>>
> >>>>> Is there some reason I should have expected this? Have I misunderstood something crucial about the noncentered approach, or about optimizing in Stan?  Below is the toy script, thanks!
> >>>>>
> >>>>>
> >>>>> hn=50
> >>>>> hmu<-2
> >>>>> hsd<-.2
> >>>>> ar=.6
> >>>>> mu<-rnorm(hn,hmu,hsd)
> >>>>>
> >>>>> n=10
> >>>>> diffusion=.4
> >>>>> x<-matrix(rnorm(hn,1/(1-ar)*hx,sqrt(diffusion^2/(1-ar^2))),ncol=hn)
> >>>>> for(i in 2:n){
> >>>>> x <- rbind(x,x[i-1,]*ar+hx+rnorm(hn,0,diffusion))
> >>>>> }
> >>>>>
> >>>>>
> >>>>> require(rstan)
> >>>>> noncentered<-"
> >>>>> data{
> >>>>> int hn;
> >>>>> int n;
> >>>>> vector[n] x[hn];
> >>>>> }
> >>>>> parameters{
> >>>>> real<lower=0.0001,upper=1> ar;
> >>>>> real<lower=.001,upper=10> diffusion;
> >>>>> real<lower=.001,upper=10> hsd;
> >>>>> real hmu;
> >>>>> vector[hn] mudev;
> >>>>> }
> >>>>> model{
> >>>>> vector[hn] mu;
> >>>>> mudev~normal(0,1);
> >>>>> mu<-hmu+mudev*hsd;
> >>>>> for(subjecti in 1:hn){
> >>>>> x[subjecti,1]~normal(1/(1-ar)*mu[subjecti],((diffusion^2)/(1-ar^2)));
> >>>>> for(rowi in 2:n) x[subjecti,rowi]~normal(mu[subjecti]+ar*x[subjecti,rowi-1],diffusion^2);
> >>>>> }
> >>>>> }"
> >>>>>
> >>>>>
> >>>>>
> >>>>> centered<-"
> >>>>> data{
> >>>>> int hn;
> >>>>> int n;
> >>>>> vector[n] x[hn];
> >>>>> }
> >>>>> parameters{
> >>>>> real<lower=0.0001,upper=1> ar;
> >>>>> real<lower=.001,upper=10> diffusion;
> >>>>> real<lower=.001,upper=10> hsd;
> >>>>> real hmu;
> >>>>> vector[hn] mu;
> >>>>> }
> >>>>> model{
> >>>>> mu~normal(hmu,hsd);
> >>>>> for(subjecti in 1:hn){
> >>>>> x[subjecti,1]~normal(1/(1-ar)*mu[subjecti],((diffusion^2)/(1-ar^2)));
> >>>>> for(rowi in 2:n) x[subjecti,rowi]~normal(mu[subjecti]+ar*x[subjecti,rowi-1],diffusion^2);
> >>>>> }
> >>>>> }"
> >>>>>
> >>>>>
> >>>>> standata<-list(x=t(x),hn=hn,n=n)
> >>>>>
> >>>>> centerednuts<-stan(model_code = c(centered),
> >>>>> data = standata, iter = 1000, chains = 3,
> >>>>> cores=3)
> >>>>> centerednuts
> >>>>>
> >>>>> noncenterednuts<-stan(model_code = c(noncentered),
> >>>>> data = standata, iter = 1000, chains = 3,
> >>>>> cores=3)
> >>>>> noncenterednuts
> >>>>>
> >>>>> centeredoptim<- optimizing(object = centerednuts@stanmodel,
> >>>>> algorithm='BFGS',
> >>>>> as_vector=F,
> >>>>> tol_obj=1e-10, tol_grad=1e-10,tol_param=1e-10,tol_rel_grad=0, tol_rel_obj=0,
> >>>>> data = standata)
> >>>>> centeredoptim
> >>>>>
> >>>>> noncenteredoptim<- optimizing(object = noncenterednuts@stanmodel,
> >>>>> algorithm='BFGS',
> >>>>> as_vector=F,
> >>>>> tol_obj=1e-10, tol_grad=1e-10,tol_param=1e-10,tol_rel_grad=0, tol_rel_obj=0,
> >>>>> data = standata)
> >>>>> noncenteredoptim
